const slides=[
[1,'Key things you need to refresh your memory about','Opening visual introducing the background knowledge needed for this course.','Visual introduction; the slide itself contains no detailed lecture text.','Image-led slide; manually inspect the original visual if you need details beyond the heading.', 'foundation'],
[2,'1.1 Before we start','Review important heterocycles, Gram-positive and Gram-negative pathogenic bacteria, and drug metabolism.','The slide lists prerequisite topics and examples of common heterocycles: epoxide, aziridine, thiirane, furan, pyrrole, thiophene, oxazole, thiazole, imidazole, tetrahydrofuran, pyrrolidine and tetrahydrothiophene.','Heterocycles; Gram-positive; Gram-negative; drug metabolism.','Use this as the prerequisite checklist before the sulfonamide content.', 'foundation'],
[3,'Examples of common heterocycles','Additional heterocyclic ring examples shown as chemical structures.','Structures labelled thiopyran, pyran, pyridine, pyrimidine, pyrazine, tetrahydrothiopyran, tetrahydropyran, piperidine, morpholine, piperazine, dioxane, thiomorpholine, azepine, 1,4-diazepine, indole, quinoline, isoquinoline, chroman, purine and pteridine.','Heterocycle names and ring structures.','Image-led structure sheet: compare ring size, heteroatom identity and fused-ring patterns visually.', 'foundation'],
[4,'Summary of phase I and II biotransformations','Drug metabolism is presented as Phase I functionalization and Phase II conjugation.','Phase I: hydrolytic reactions (esters, amides, epoxides and arene oxides by epoxide hydrase); oxidation (aromatic moieties, olefins, benzylic/allylic C atoms, α-C of C=O and C=N, aliphatic/alicyclic C, C-N, C-O and C-S systems, alcohols and aldehydes); reduction (aldehydes, ketones, nitro and azo groups). Phase II: glucuronic acid, sulfate, glycine and other amino acids, glutathione or mercapturic acid conjugation, acetylation and methylation.','Phase I; functionalization; hydrolysis; oxidation; reduction; Phase II; conjugation.','This slide is a broad review map, not a sulfonamide-specific metabolic pathway.', 'foundation'],
[5,'Before the beginning of the 20th century','Question prompt: What was the average life expectancy, and why?','The slide presents a discussion question only; no numerical answer is supplied in the lecture slide.','Life expectancy; infectious disease context.','The source does not provide an answer or value. Do not infer one as lecture content.', 'foundation'],
[6,'Anti-Infective Drugs','Section divider introducing anti-infective drugs.','The slide is an image-based title card: “Anti-Infective Drugs”.','Anti-infective drugs.','Image-led section divider.', 'foundation'],
[7,'What’s the difference between antibiotic vs antibacterial?','Question prompt distinguishing antibiotic and antibacterial.','The slide asks the question but does not provide a written definition or answer.','Antibiotic; antibacterial.','The source does not define the distinction on this slide.', 'foundation'],
[8,'Antibacterial','Image-led slide labelled “Antibacterial”.','The slide contains a visual of bacteria and the word “Antibacterial”; no additional explanatory text is provided.','Antibacterial.','Image-led concept slide.', 'foundation'],
[9,'Sulfonamides','Section title introducing sulfonamides.','The slide is a visual title card labelled “Sulfonamides”.','Sulfonamides.','Image-led section divider.', 'intro'],
[10,'Sulfonamides: Prontosil Rubrum','Prontosil Rubrum is shown as inactive in vitro but active in vivo; treated-animal urine was bioactive in vitro.','The diagram shows liver reduction [H] converting Prontosil Rubrum to sulfanilamide. The slide describes sulfanilamide as the first synthetic antibacterial agent. It also identifies Gerhard Johannes Paul Domagk and the discovery of sulfonamidochrysoidine (Prontosil).','Prontosil Rubrum; sulfanilamide; liver reduction; inactive in vitro; active in vivo.','The prodrug-like conversion shown is a central historical concept in this lecture.', 'intro'],
[11,'Mechanism of action','Sulfonamides interfere with the microbial biosynthetic pathway leading to tetrahydrofolic acid synthesis.','The pathway diagram shows dihydroneopterin diphosphate and p-aminobenzoic acid (PABA) feeding into dihydropteroic acid via dihydropteroate synthase. Sulfonamides are shown at this step and a false metabolite is illustrated. The downstream pathway leads to tetrahydrofolic acid and then thymidine/DNA.','PABA; dihydropteroate synthase (DHPS); dihydropteroic acid; tetrahydrofolic acid; false metabolite.','Lecture diagram: competitive interference at the PABA-utilizing DHPS step.', 'mechanism'],
[12,'DHPS binding environment — research figure','Research visual showing the enzyme active-site environment and ligand interactions.','The slide cites a research figure (Future Medicinal Chemistry, Vol. 10, No. 8; citation text also appears as “J. Name., 2012, 00, 1–3”). The image includes protein/ligand binding representations.','DHPS active site; ligand binding.','Image-led scientific figure; inspect the original page for atomic-level details.', 'mechanism'],
[13,'Recognize CADD and AI tools','Prompt to recognize computer-aided drug design (CADD) and AI tools.','The slide contains the heading “Recognize CADD and AI tools” and a visual “think up” graphic.','CADD; AI tools.','No specific software names or tool functions are listed in the source.', 'mechanism'],
[14,'DHPS structural panels and colour scheme','The figure explains the protein, amino-acid, ligand and atom representations and describes reaction panels.','Protein is shown in cartoon form (α-helices and loops). Interacting amino acids are shown as sticks. Ligands are ball-and-stick; Mg²⁺ ions are black spheres. Atom colours: N blue, O red, S yellow. Panel B: enzyme–substrate complex immediately before nucleophilic attack by pABA nitrogen on DHP+ carbon, after PPi release. Panel C: enzyme bound to dihydropteroate. Panel D: SMX occupies the pABA binding site; slide text calls it a competitive, reversible inhibitor of dihydrofolate reductase.','pABA; SMX; Mg²⁺; PPi; nucleophilic attack; DHP+; dihydropteroate.','Source wording on Panel D is preserved as written; see the mechanism pathway slide for the DHPS context.', 'mechanism'],
[15,'Mechanism of action of sulfonamides','Visual summary of sulfonamide binding to dihydropteroate synthase and inhibition of folic acid synthesis.','The infographic shows sulfonamide binding to dihydropteroate synthase, inhibition of folic acid synthesis, decreased DNA and RNA production, and inhibition of bacterial growth.','DHPS; folic acid synthesis; DNA; RNA; bacterial growth.','Image-led summary of the mechanism shown in slide 11.', 'mechanism'],
[16,'Why sulfonamides are selectively toxic?','The slide contrasts human folate acquisition with bacterial folate synthesis.','Human: “Folate from diet”. Bacteria: “Make their own folate”.','Selective toxicity; dietary folate; bacterial folate synthesis.','The visual presents the basis of selectivity as a difference in folate source.', 'mechanism'],
[17,'Intrinsic resistance to sulfonamides','Question prompt: Some bacteria are intrinsically resistant to sulfonamides — how?','The slide asks the question and does not provide an answer.','Intrinsic resistance; sulfonamides.','No resistance mechanism is specified in the source slide.', 'mechanism'],
[18,'Binding interactions','The slide compares binding interactions and pKa values for PABA-like and sulfonamide structures.','The interaction key lists H-bond, van der Waals interactions and ionic bond. The slide asks the type of inhibition and how potency could be enhanced. Structures are annotated pKa 4.9 and pKa 10.4.','Hydrogen bond; van der Waals; ionic bond; pKa 4.9; pKa 10.4.','The slide is a question-led medicinal chemistry comparison; do not infer a potency value.', 'physchem'],
[19,'Sulfonamide pKa modification','Examples show sulfisoxazole, its sodium salt, sulfathiazole and sulfadiazine-related modification.','Sulfisoxazole and Na sulfisoxazole are shown with pKa ≈ 5.0. An electron-withdrawing group (EWG) is highlighted. The slide asks why these modifications reduce toxicity (crystalluria).','EWG; pKa 5; sulfisoxazole; sodium salt; sulfathiazole; sulfadiazine; crystalluria.','The figure links structural/pKa changes with ionization and crystalluria; slide 22 supplies the explanatory text.', 'physchem'],
[20,'Sulfathiazole to sulfadiazine — annotated structures','Hand-annotated structural comparison and heteroaromatic ring changes.','The page shows sulfathiazole and sulfadiazine structures with handwritten annotations, including “Diazine” and pKa 6.48 / 86% ionized on the following structure.','Sulfathiazole; sulfadiazine; diazine; pKa.','Handwritten annotations are image-based; interpret cautiously and refer to the page image.', 'physchem'],
[21,'Crystalluria and pKa range','Crystalluria is associated with un-ionized drug at urine pH values; maximal activity is stated for pKa 6.6–7.4.','The slide contrasts sulfonamide pKa 10.4 and pKa 5 and states: “Crystalluria → un-ionized at urine pH values”. It states maximal activity seems to be exhibited by sulfonamides between pKa 6.6 and 7.4. A pH scale illustrates ionized/un-ionized forms.','Crystalluria; urine pH; pKa 6.6–7.4; ionization.','Keep the stated range as lecture content; the explanatory paragraph is on slide 22.', 'physchem'],
[22,'Solubility, ionization and crystalluria','Poor water solubility of early sulfonamides caused occasional crystalluria because they were un-ionized at urine pH.','More important agents used today form sodium salts that are at least partly ionized and reasonably water soluble at urinary pH. Sulfisoxazole pKa ≈ 5.0. Maximal activity seems to occur between pKa 6.6 and 7.4, reflecting the need for enough nonionized (more lipid-soluble) drug at physiological pH to pass through bacterial cell walls. Historically, increased water intake or carbonate to increase urine pH was recommended to decrease crystalluria.','Water solubility; sodium salts; urinary pH; nonionized fraction; lipid solubility; pKa.','Source-derived explanation. The lecture describes a balance between aqueous solubility and nonionized drug available for cell-wall passage.', 'physchem'],
[23,'Activity, potency and solubility examples','A structure panel classifies examples as active, inactive or active in vivo only and indicates potency/solubility comparisons.','The slide labels multiple structures Active, Inactive, or Active in vivo only. Two arrow comparisons are labelled “Potency” and “Solubility”.','Active; inactive; active in vivo only; potency; solubility.','Image-based SAR panel: the exact structural distinctions should be read from the original structures, not inferred from labels alone.', 'sar'],
[24,'Structure–activity relationships','The slide identifies essential features of the sulfonamide pharmacophore and effects of varying R.','Aromatic ring is essential and para-substituted only. N4 is essential for activity and must be unsubstituted. Sulfonamide is essential and N1 nitrogen must be secondary. R1=acyl are allowed; inactive in vitro, active in vivo. Varying R can affect pharmacokinetics: solubility and plasma protein binding. The slide asks how R could increase water solubility.','Aromatic ring; para substitution; N4; N1; secondary sulfonamide; R1-acyl; solubility; plasma protein binding.','These are the slide’s stated SAR rules; preserve their wording when revising.', 'sar'],
[25,'Question','A question slide between SAR and pharmacokinetics.','The slide contains only the heading “Question”; no readable question text is present.','Question.','Image-only/blank question page; manual inspection required.', 'sar'],
[26,'Pharmacokinetics: Metabolism','Sulfonamides undergo N-acetylation; this increases hydrophobic character and reduces aqueous solubility.','Sulfathiazole is shown undergoing N-acetylation to an insoluble metabolite. The slide states this may cause toxic side effects (renal tubule blockage). Orally administered sulfonamides are well absorbed from the GI tract, distributed fairly widely and excreted by the kidney. They bind plasma protein and may displace other protein-bound drugs and bilirubin; therefore they are not used in late-term pregnancy because they can cause neonatal jaundice.','N-acetylation; insoluble metabolite; renal tubule blockage; GI absorption; kidney excretion; plasma protein binding; bilirubin; neonatal jaundice.','All statements above are transcribed from the lecture slide.', 'pk'],
[27,'Therapeutic applications','Only a few sulfonamides remain available and are often used in combination with other agents.','Sulfisoxazole; sulfamethoxazole; sulfadiazine used topically for burns. Sulfacetamide is used ophthalmically for eye infections. The slide explanation says ophthalmic use needs high water solubility (salt form); lower pKa/more acidic sulfonamide is associated with C=O as a better EWG, more electronegative and close to NH (inductive and resonance), so its salt will not precipitate. Sulfadoxine is described as long-acting with once-weekly dosing and very high protein binding.','Sulfisoxazole; sulfamethoxazole; sulfadiazine; sulfacetamide; sulfadoxine; ophthalmic; topical; protein binding.','The sulfacetamide explanation is retained in the lecturer’s wording and is not independently corrected here.', 'applications'],
[28,'Prodrugs of sulfonamides','The slide relates lipophilicity to absorption and illustrates prodrugs designed for local gut action.','“More lipophilic >> Better absorption”. Succinyl sulfathiazole is converted by enzyme to succinic acid and sulfathiazole; it has local effect in gut and is too polar to cross the gut wall. Benzoyl prodrug is converted to benzoic acid and sulfonamide; it has local effect in gut and is too hydrophobic to cross the gut wall.','Prodrug; lipophilicity; absorption; succinyl sulfathiazole; benzoyl prodrug; enzyme cleavage.','The slide contrasts two extremes of polarity/lipophilicity that limit gut-wall passage.', 'applications'],
[29,'Prodrugs of sulfonamides: sulfasalazine','Sulfasalazine is shown as a prodrug that is cleaved in the gut.','The diagram shows sulfasalazine undergoing reductive cleavage in the gut [H] to 5-aminosalicylic acid (Mesalamine), labelled anti-inflammatory agent, and sulfapyridine.','Sulfasalazine; gut; reduction; 5-aminosalicylic acid; mesalamine; sulfapyridine.','Image-based reaction scheme; products and anti-inflammatory label are taken from the slide.', 'applications'],
[30,'Dihydrofolate reductase inhibitor','The folate pathway includes dihydrofolate reductase converting dihydrofolic acid to tetrahydrofolic acid.','The pathway diagram shows dihydropteroic acid and dietary folic acid leading to dihydrofolic acid; dihydrofolate reductase is the next enzyme, and trimethoprim is shown inhibiting this step. Tetrahydrofolic acid is downstream.','Dihydrofolic acid; dihydrofolate reductase (DHFR); trimethoprim; tetrahydrofolic acid.','The diagram presents the second blockade point in the folate pathway.', 'combination'],
[31,'Trimethoprim','Trimethoprim is shown with structural fragments and selective toxicity.','The slide labels pyrimidine-2,4-diamine and 3,4,5-trimethoxyphenyl)methyl fragments. It shows inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase and states bacterial enzyme is 100,000 times more sensitive.','Trimethoprim; DHFR; selective toxicity; 100,000 times; pyrimidine-2,4-diamine; trimethoxyphenyl.','The 100,000-fold sensitivity statement is lecture content.', 'combination'],
[32,'TMP-SMX: combined folate pathway blockade','The combination blocks sequential steps in folate synthesis.','The slide identifies TMP-SMX as trimethoprim/sulfamethoxazole. Sulfonamides inhibit dihydropteroate synthase; trimethoprim inhibits dihydrofolate reductase. It states both medicines alone are bacteriostatic, but when combined they are bactericidal.','TMP-SMX; cotrimoxazole; DHPS; DHFR; bacteriostatic; bactericidal.','This is the combination mechanism summary as presented by the lecture.', 'combination'],
[33,'Comparative analysis: sulfonamides alone vs cotrimoxazole','A comparison table contrasts sulfonamide monotherapy with cotrimoxazole (SMX + TMP).','Mechanism: DHPS inhibition vs dual blockade (folate pathway). Effect: bacteriostatic vs bactericidal. Spectrum: narrower vs broader. Clinical use: limited vs common in UTIs, PJP and toxoplasmosis.','Sulfonamide alone; cotrimoxazole; dual blockade; spectrum; UTIs; PJP; toxoplasmosis.','Table transcribed from the final slide.', 'combination']
].map(x=>({n:x[0],title:x[1],concept:x[2],content:x[3],terms:x[4],note:x[5],module:x[6]}));
